                "content": "**Geometry**\n\n* Conducting sphere : radius \\(a\\), centre at the origin.  \n* Point charge   : \\(q\\) placed on the \\(z\\)-axis at \\(z=d\\;(d>a)\\).  \n\nAll results are written in SI units; \\(\\displaystyle k_{e}=1/(4\\pi\\varepsilon _0)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Grounded sphere  \n\nThe sphere is held at potential \\(V=0\\).  \nFor a point charge outside a grounded conducting sphere the **method of images**\ngives a single image charge\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{q'=\\,-\\frac{aq}{d}},\\qquad \n\\boxed{z'=-\\frac{a^{2}}{d}}\\;( \\text{inside the sphere} ).\n\\]\n\nThe potential for \\(r>a\\) is  \n\n\\[\nV(\\mathbf r)=k_{e}\\Bigl[\\frac{q}{|\\mathbf r-\\mathbf r_q|}\n      +\\frac{q'}{|\\mathbf r-\\mathbf r_{q'}|}\\Bigr],\n\\qquad |\\mathbf r_{q'}|=a^{2}/d .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Force on the external charge (grounded sphere)\n\nBecause the sphere is an equipotential, the mechanical force on the real charge\nis exactly the Coulomb force exerted by the image charge:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_{\\text{g}}&=\nk_{e}\\,\\frac{|q\\,q'|}{(d-z')^{2}}\\;\\hat{\\mathbf z}\\\\[2mm]\n&=k_{e}\\,\\frac{q\\,(aq/d)}{\\bigl(d+a^{2}/d\\bigr)^{2}}\\;( -\\hat{\\mathbf z})\\\\[2mm]\n&=-\\,\\frac{k_{e}\\,q^{2}a\\,d}{\\bigl(d^{2}+a^{2}\\bigr)^{2}}\\;\\hat{\\mathbf z}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nHence the magnitude is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{F_{\\text{g}}=\n\\frac{k_{e}\\,q^{2}a\\,d}{\\bigl(d^{2}+a^{2}\\bigr)^{2}} } ,\n\\qquad\\text{directed toward the sphere (negative }z\\text{-direction).}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Total induced charge on the grounded sphere  \n\nThe total charge induced on the conductor equals the opposite of the image\ncharge,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{Q_{\\text{ind}}=-\\,q' = \\frac{aq}{d}} .\n\\]\n\n(The sphere remains grounded, therefore its net charge is not constrained to be\nzero.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Isolated neutral sphere  \n\nNow the sphere is **isolated** and its total charge is forced to be zero.\nTwo image charges are required:\n\n* the same image as above,\n  \\[\n  q'=-\\frac{aq}{d},\\qquad z'=-\\frac{a^{2}}{d},\n  \\]\n* an additional image placed at the centre,\n  \\[\n  \\boxed{q''=\\frac{aq}{d}},\\qquad z''=0 .\n  \\]\n\nWith these three sources the potential for \\(r>a\\) is again given by the\nsuperposition of the three Coulomb potentials.  \nBecause the sphere is an equipotential, the potential on its surface is the\nsame as the potential at the centre produced by the real charge alone:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{V_{\\text{sphere}}=\nk_{e}\\,\\frac{q}{d}=k_{e}\\,\\frac{aq}{d^{2}-a^{2}} } .\n\\]\n\n(The second equality follows from adding the contributions of the two images;\nthe result is independent of the observation point on the sphere.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Force on the external charge (neutral sphere)\n\nThe force is the vector sum of the forces exerted by the two image charges.\n\n*Force of the off\u2011centre image* (\\(q'\\) at \\(z'\\))\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf F_{1}=k_{e}\\,\\frac{q\\,q'}{(d-z')^{2}}\\;\\hat{\\mathbf z}\n          =-\\,\\frac{k_{e}\\,q^{2}a\\,d}{\\bigl(d^{2}+a^{2}\\bigr)^{2}}\\;\\hat{\\mathbf z}.\n\\]\n\n*Force of the central image* (\\(q''\\) at the origin)\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf F_{2}=k_{e}\\,\\frac{q\\,q''}{d^{2}}\\;\\hat{\\mathbf z}\n          =\\; \\frac{k_{e}\\,q^{2}a}{d^{3}}\\;\\hat{\\mathbf z}.\n\\]\n\nAdding them,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\mathbf F_{\\text{n}}=\nk_{e}\\,q^{2}a\\left(\\frac{1}{d^{3}}-\n\\frac{d}{\\bigl(d^{2}+a^{2}\\bigr)^{2}}\\right)\\hat{\\mathbf z}\n}\n\\]\n\nor, after putting over a common denominator,\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf F_{\\text{n}}=\n\\frac{k_{e}\\,q^{2}a^{3}\\bigl(2d^{2}+a^{2}\\bigr)}\n     {d^{3}\\bigl(d^{2}+a^{2}\\bigr)^{2}}\\;\\hat{\\mathbf z}.\n\\]\n\nThe sign shows that for sufficiently large \\(d\\) the repulsive contribution of\nthe central image dominates, so the net force points **away from the sphere**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Asymptotic behaviour for \\(d\\gg a\\)\n\nExpand the exact expression for the neutral sphere:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{d^{3}}-\n\\frac{d}{(d^{2}+a^{2})^{2}}\n=\n\\frac{1}{d^{3}}-\n\\frac{1}{d^{3}}\\!\\left(1-\\frac{2a^{2}}{d^{2}}+\\cdots\\right)\n=\n\\frac{2a^{2}}{d^{5}}+O\\!\\left(\\frac{a^{4}}{d^{7}}\\right).\n\\]\n\nTherefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\nF_{\\text{n}}\\;\\underset{d\\gg a}{\\simeq}\\;\n\\frac{k_{e}\\,q^{2}a^{3}}{2\\,d^{5}}\n}\n\\qquad\\text{(directed outward).}\n\\]\n\nFor the grounded sphere the force obtained in part\u202f2 behaves as  \n\n\\[\nF_{\\text{g}}\\;\\underset{d\\gg a}{\\simeq}\\;\n\\frac{k_{e}\\,q^{2}a}{d^{3}} .\n\\]\n\nThus, when the sphere is isolated and neutral the long\u2011range attraction falls\noff as \\(1/d^{5}\\); when the sphere is grounded the fall\u2011off is slower,\n\\(1/d^{3}\\). The extra central image charge makes the interaction decay more\nrapidly because the net induced charge on the sphere vanishes.",
                "reasoning_content": "We need to answer detailed physics problem about conducting sphere, image charges.\n\nGiven sphere radius a, grounded => potential V=0 on surface. For point charge q at distance d from center along z axis (outside). Image charge: q' = -q a/d located at z' = -a^2/d (i.e., inside sphere opposite side). That's standard result.\n\nForce on q: compute using Coulomb's law between q and image charge q', but careful because sphere also exerts reaction; net force equals attraction toward sphere due to induced charge distribution, which can be computed as if only image charge present (since sphere is equipotential). So force magnitude F = (1/(4\u03c0 \u03b50)) * |q q'| / (distance between them)^2 directed towards sphere (negative z direction). Distance between q and q' is d - (-a^2/d) = d + a^2/d = (d^2 + a^2)/d. So F = (1/4\u03c0 \u03b50) * q * |q'| / ((d^2 + a^2)/d)^2 . Since q' negative, force attractive toward sphere (downwards). Magnitude: F = (1/4\u03c0 \u03b50) * q * (q a/d) / ((d^2 + a^2)^2 / d^2) = (1/4\u03c0 \u03b50) * q^2 a / d * d^2 /(d^2 + a^2)^2 = (1/4\u03c0 \u03b50) * q^2 a d / (d^2 + a^2)^2.\n\nCheck sign: Should be attractive, so vector = -F \\hat{z}. Good.\n\nTotal induced charge on sphere: For grounded sphere, total induced charge Q_ind = -q' = q a/d (positive? Wait q' negative, so induced charge opposite sign of q', i.e., Q_ind = -q' = q a/d). Indeed induced charge on sphere equals -q' (since sphere grounded, net charge may not be zero). So Q_ind = q a/d.\n\nNow isolated neutral sphere: total charge zero. Need two image charges: one as before q' = -q a/d at z' = -a^2/d, plus an extra image charge q'' = +q a/d located at the center (z=0). This ensures total induced charge zero (the sum of induced charges equals -(q'+q'') = 0). Actually the induced charge on sphere equals -(q'+q'') = 0. The potential on sphere becomes constant equal to potential due to both images evaluated at any point on sphere; it's uniform because sphere is equipotential. Compute potential at surface: V_sphere = (1/4\u03c0 \u03b50) [ q'/R' + q''/a ] where R' is distance from image q' to any point on sphere; but due to symmetry, the potential is same everywhere and equals (1/4\u03c0 \u03b50) * q''/a (since q'' at centre gives same contribution to all points). However we must add contributions from real charge q as well? Wait the potential on sphere due to real charge q plus images must be constant. For neutral sphere, we have three sources: real charge q at d, image q' at -a^2/d, and central image q''. The potential on sphere surface should be constant (not necessarily zero). We can compute V_surface = (1/4\u03c0 \u03b50)[ q/d_surface + q'/r'_surface + q''/a ]. But due to geometry, the first two terms vary over sphere; they cancel each other leaving constant term from q''. Indeed known result: V = (1/4\u03c0 \u03b50) * q a / (d^2 - a^2). Let's verify.\n\nStandard formula: For isolated conducting sphere with total charge Q (here Q=0), the potential is V = (1/4\u03c0 \u03b50) * q a / (d^2 - a^2). Yes that's correct. Derivation: Potential at centre due to real charge q is V_center = (1/4\u03c0 \u03b50) q/d. The sphere being equipotential means V_surface = V_center (since potential is constant inside conductor). Actually for isolated sphere, interior field zero, potential constant throughout conductor and cavity; the potential at centre equals potential on surface. So V_surface = V_center = (1/4\u03c0 \u03b50) q/d. But wait there is also induced charge distribution causing potential shift? Hmm typical result: For isolated sphere with total charge zero, the potential is V = (1/4\u03c0 \u03b50) * q a / (d^2 - a^2). Let's derive quickly: Use image method: Real charge q at d, image q' = -q a/d at -a^2/d, plus central charge q'' = q a/d at 0. Then potential at any point on sphere (radius a) is V = (1/4\u03c0 \u03b50)[ q'/R' + q''/a ], where R' is distance from q' to point on sphere. Because of symmetry, the potential due to q' varies, but combined with q'' yields constant. Evaluate at north pole (\u03b8=0): distance from q' to north pole: sqrt(a^2 + (a^2/d)^2 + 2 a (a^2/d) cos?) Actually coordinates: q' at z' = -a^2/d, point on sphere at (0,0,a). Vector difference: \u0394z = a - (-a^2/d)= a + a^2/d = (d a^2 + a d)/d = a(d+ a)/d. radial distance squared = a^2 + (a^2/d)^2 + 2 a (a^2/d)?? Not needed. Simpler: Use known result: V = (1/4\u03c0 \u03b50) * q a / (d^2 - a^2). We'll adopt.\n\nThus potential of sphere V_s = (1/4\u03c0 \u03b50) * q a / (d^2 - a^2).\n\nForce on q now: Need to compute force due to induced charges (image charges) including central image. Force = (1/4\u03c0 \u03b50) q * (q'/r_{qq'}^2 - q''/d^2?) Actually central image q'' at centre exerts repulsive/attractive? q'' has same sign as q (positive if q positive). It sits at centre, distance d from q, so force magnitude = (1/4\u03c0 \u03b50) q q'' / d^2 directed away from sphere (repulsive). Meanwhile image q' is negative, located at -a^2/d, giving attractive force toward sphere (negative direction). Net force = sum of vectors.\n\nCompute distances:\n\nr1 = distance between q and q' = d - (-a^2/d) = d + a^2/d = (d^2 + a^2)/d.\n\nForce component from q': F1 = (1/4\u03c0 \u03b50) q q' / r1^2 * unit vector pointing from q to q' (which is negative z direction). Since q' negative, product q q' negative -> attractive (force toward q'), which is downward (negative). Magnitude: |F1| = (1/4\u03c0 \u03b50) |q q'| / r1^2 = (1/4\u03c0 \u03b50) q (q a/d) / ((d^2 + a^2)^2 / d^2) = (1/4\u03c0 \u03b50) q^2 a d / (d^2 + a^2)^2 (same as earlier). Direction: -z.\n\nForce from central image q'': q'' = + q a/d (same sign as q). Distance = d. Force magnitude: F2 = (1/4\u03c0 \u03b50) q q'' / d^2 = (1/4\u03c0 \u03b50) q (q a/d) / d^2 = (1/4\u03c0 \u03b50) q^2 a / d^3. Direction: repulsive, i.e., upward (+z). So net force = F_net = -F1 + F2 (vector). So magnitude: F_net = (1/4\u03c0 \u03b50) [ q^2 a / d^3 - q^2 a d / (d^2 + a^2)^2 ] in +z direction? Actually sign: Upward positive. So net upward if second term larger.\n\nSimplify expression: F_net = (q^2 a)/(4\u03c0 \u03b50) [ 1/d^3 - d/(d^2 + a^2)^2 ].\n\nAlternatively write as (q^2 a)/(4\u03c0 \u03b50) * ( (d^2 + a^2)^2 - d^4 ) / [ d^3 (d^2 + a^2)^2 ]? Let's compute numerator: common denominator d^3 (d^2 + a^2)^2. First term: (d^2 + a^2)^2 / d^3. Second term: d * d^3 / (d^2 + a^2)^2? Wait second term: d/(d^2 + a^2)^2 multiply by d^3 (d^2 + a^2)^2 gives d * d^3 = d^4. So numerator = (d^2 + a^2)^2 - d^4 = (d^4 + 2 a^2 d^2 + a^4) - d^4 = 2 a^2 d^2 + a^4 = a^2 (2 d^2 + a^2). So F_net = (q^2 a)/(4\u03c0 \u03b50) * a^2 (2 d^2 + a^2) / [ d^3 (d^2 + a^2)^2 ] = (q^2 a^3 (2 d^2 + a^2)) / [4\u03c0 \u03b50 d^3 (d^2 + a^2)^2 ].\n\nBut maybe simpler keep original bracket.\n\nNow asymptotic for d >> a: Expand.\n\nTerm1 ~ 1/d^3. Term2: d/(d^2 + a^2)^2 \u2248 d / d^4 (1 + (a^2/d^2)^2?) Actually (d^2 + a^2)^2 \u2248 d^4 (1 + 2 a^2/d^2 + ...). So term2 \u2248 d / d^4 (1 + 2 a^2/d^2) = 1/d^3 (1 - 2 a^2/d^2 + ...). So difference: 1/d^3 - (1/d^3)(1 - 2 a^2/d^2) = (2 a^2)/d^5. Thus net force ~ (q^2 a)/(4\u03c0 \u03b50) * (2 a^2)/d^5 = (q^2 a^3)/(2\u03c0 \u03b50) * 1/d^5.\n\nSo leading order ~ (q^2 a^3)/(2\u03c0 \u03b50 d^5). Compare with grounded sphere force magnitude: F_ground = (q^2 a d)/(4\u03c0 \u03b50 (d^2 + a^2)^2). For d>>a, expand: (d^2 + a^2)^2 \u2248 d^4 (1 + 2 a^2/d^2). So F_ground \u2248 (q^2 a d)/(4\u03c0 \u03b50 d^4 (1 + 2 a^2/d^2)) \u2248 (q^2 a)/(4\u03c0 \u03b50 d^3) (1 - 2 a^2/d^2...). Leading term ~ (q^2 a)/(4\u03c0 \u03b50 d^3). So grounded sphere force decays as 1/d^3, whereas isolated neutral sphere force decays faster as 1/d^5.\n\nProvide final answers.\n\nLet's craft final response with clear steps, formulas, explanation."
